        "text": "Report Presentation\nLet\u2019s look at how to create a professional report based on examples so you\ncan do the same on your end. In general, four inputs are feeding our reports (I\nuse the report generated by the tools to create and extract\u2014not blindly\u2014the\ninformation for my final report.):\nI\nI Infrastructure flaws found by a scanner (e.g., OpenVAS, Nessus, Qualys,\netc.) or manual findings\nI\nI Web application flaws found by a scanner (e.g., Burp Suite) or manual\nfindings\nI\nI Source code flaws found by a static application security testing (SAST)\ntool (e.g., Checkmarx, Veracode, etc.) or findings using a manual code\nreview\nI\nI Open source library flaws found by a library scanner (e.g., Nexus Sonatype\nlifecycle)\nFigure 14.2: CVSS Results\nTable 14.1: CVSS Score Rating\nRATING\nCVSS 3.1 SCORE\nNone\n0\nLow\n0.1\u20133.9\nMedium\n4.0\u20136.9\nHigh\n7.0\u20138.9\nCritical\n9.0\u201310.0\n350\nChapter 14 I Reporting\nHere\u2019s a report structure that you can use to create a report on your end.\nGenerally, each company has its own logo and templates for the look and feel.\nEsta secci\u00f3n contiene las siguientes subsecciones\n\u2022 Cover Page\n\u2022 History Logs\n\u2022 Report Summary\n\u2022 Vulnerabilities Section\nSi deseas explorar cualquiera de estas subsecciones, puedes pregunt\u00e1rmelo directamente.",
